from lib import *

# ---------------- PRINCIPLE 1 : CONSENT
slide("Principle 1 · Consent", "A &#8220;yes&#8221; needs knowledge behind it",
'''<div class="card dash"><b>Imagine</b> a baker who mixes an ingredient, not on the label, into his bread so that it goes stale in a day. You paid for bread that keeps. Tomorrow you must buy again. Would anyone call that a fair sale?</div>
<div class="cols" style="margin-top:20px"><div class="col"><div class="label">The norm</div><div class="bar" style="font-size:27px"><b>N1.</b> Wealth passes from one person to another only by informed consent.</div>
<p class="small" style="margin-top:16px">&#8220;Informed&#8221; means the buyer knew the facts that set the price, or could not have missed them.</p></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">Try to deny it</div><ul class="b small">
<li>Then a seller may promise one thing and hand over another, and the payment still counts as agreed.</li>
<li>Then a price tells the buyer nothing about what he gets.</li>
<li>Then no one could rationally pay in advance for anything.</li></ul>
<p class="small"><b>So N1 is not an extra rule laid on trade. It is what makes a price mean something.</b> Whoever denies it has already stopped trading.</p></div></div>''')

slide("Principle 1 · Consent", "Proof: concealing the limit takes part of the price without consent",
'''<ol class="prem" style="margin-bottom:12px">
<li data-n="1.1">A buyer pays a price because he believes the product will give him a benefit for a time <i>E</i>.</li>
<li data-n="1.2">The maker knows the product will give that benefit only for <i>L</i>, and <i>L</i> &lt; <i>E</i>. <span class="tiny">(fact F1)</span></li>
<li data-n="1.3">The buyer formed <i>E</i> from what such products ordinarily give, and from the maker&#8217;s silence. <span class="tiny">(F2, F3)</span></li>
<li data-n="1.4">So the buyer agreed to pay for <i>E</i>. He did not agree to pay for <i>L</i>. <span class="tiny">(from 1.1, 1.3)</span></li>
<li data-n="1.5">Wealth taken beyond what was agreed is taken wrongly. <span class="tiny">(N1; Qur&#8217;an 4:29)</span></li>
<li class="concl" data-n="1.6">Therefore the part <i>P</i>&nbsp;(1&nbsp;−&nbsp;<i>L</i>/<i>E</i>) was taken wrongly. <span style="float:right">'''+chip("certain")+'''</span></li></ol>
<table style="font-size:19px"><tr><th style="width:70px">Leaf</th><th>What the buyer expects (<i>E</i>)</th><th>What the maker delivers (<i>L</i>)</th></tr>
<tr><td class="k">A1</td><td>The ordinary life of such a device</td><td>The designed life</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">A2</td><td>The performance he bought</td><td>The performance after the update</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">B</td><td>Support as long as the device itself works</td><td>Support until the maker&#8217;s chosen date</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">C</td><td>The power to repair what he owns</td><td>A repair path closed by the maker</td></tr></table>''',
"Certain <i>given</i> the facts F1 to F3. Whether a particular case has those facts is a matter of evidence, not of principle.")

slide("Principle 1 · Consent", "What the classical texts say",
'''<table style="font-size:20px"><tr><th style="width:560px">Text</th><th style="width:300px">Source and grade</th><th>What it settles</th></tr>
<tr><td><i class="t">Do not consume one another&#8217;s wealth unjustly, but only in trade by mutual consent.</i></td><td>Qur&#8217;an 4:29; see also 2:188</td><td>Wealth moves by consent.</td></tr>
<tr><td><i class="t">Whoever deceives us is not one of us.</i><br><span class="ar" style="font-size:24px;display:block;text-align:left;direction:rtl">وَمَنْ غَشَّنَا فَلَيْسَ مِنَّا</span></td><td>Sahih Muslim 101 (Abū Hurayra). Sahih</td><td>Deceit in dealings is a grave wrong.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The Prophet found wet grain under dry grain and said: <i class="t">&#8220;Why did you not put it on top, so that people could see it? Whoever deceives is not of me.&#8221;</i></td><td>Sahih Muslim 102 (Abū Hurayra). Sahih</td><td>What a buyer would care to see may not be hidden, even if he could not have found it himself.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Buyer and seller keep the option until they part; <i class="t">if they are truthful and make clear (the defects), their sale is blessed; if they lie and conceal, its blessing is erased.</i></td><td>Sahih al-Bukhārī 2079 (Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām). Sahih</td><td>Disclosure is the condition of a sound sale.</td></tr>
<tr><td><i class="t">It is not lawful for a Muslim to sell his brother goods with a defect without pointing it out.</i></td><td>Sunan Ibn Mājah 2246 (ʿUqba ibn ʿĀmir). Sahih (al-Albānī; Darussalam)</td><td>The duty to disclose rests on the seller.</td></tr></table>
<p class="small" style="margin-top:12px">'''+chip("strong")+' <b>Why not certain?</b> The texts are sound and explicit about deceit and defects. Applying them to a hidden <i>limit on life</i> is the step that needs the next two slides.</p>',
"Texts checked on prophetmuhammad.com, ihadis.com and islamtics.com; quotations are translations, shortened where marked. Qur&#8217;an 4:29 and 2:188 checked on quran.com.")

slide("Principle 1 · Consent", "What the jurists and the Mecelle add",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col"><div class="label">The rulings of the schools</div><ul class="b small">
<li>Gain obtained by <i class="t">tadlīs</i> or <i class="t">ghish</i> (hiding a defect, or showing a quality that is not there) is forbidden gain. <span class="tiny">(TDV, &#8220;Tağrîr&#8221;)</span></li>
<li>The deceived buyer may rescind (Mālikīs, Shāfiʿīs, Ḥanbalīs and others). Abū Ḥanīfa and Muḥammad prefer compensation for the loss. <span class="tiny">(TDV, &#8220;Hile&#8221;)</span></li>
<li>The ruler may punish the deceiver by <i class="t">taʿzīr</i>; scholars agree on this. <span class="tiny">(TDV, &#8220;Hile&#8221;)</span></li>
<li>A <i>defect</i> (<i class="t">ʿayb</i>) is what lowers the value of the goods in the eyes of the experts (Mecelle 338). The Shāfiʿīs also count a shortfall that frustrates the buyer&#8217;s legitimate purpose. <span class="tiny">(TDV, &#8220;Ayıp&#8221;)</span></li></ul></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">The Mecelle&#8217;s maxims on the same point</div><ul class="b small">
<li><b>Art. 67.</b> No statement is attributed to a silent person, but silence at the place where need calls for speech is a statement.</li>
<li><b>Art. 43&#8211;44.</b> What is known by custom is as if stipulated; what is known among merchants binds them as if agreed.</li>
<li><b>Art. 3.</b> In contracts, meaning counts, not wording.</li></ul>
<div class="bar" style="margin-top:14px;font-size:22px">A hidden limit on life lowers the value in expert eyes, frustrates the purpose of buying, and is silence where speech was needed. It meets all three tests.</div></div></div>''',
"Mecelle article numbers and wording: Ali Hikmet Berki&#8217;s annotated edition as reproduced by M. Balcı; art. 338 and the ayb rules: TDV İslâm Ansiklopedisi, &#8220;Ayıp&#8221; (md. 336&#8211;355).")

slide("Principle 1 · Consent", "From the udder to the device: the classical analogy",
'''<div class="cols" style="gap:24px"><div class="col"><div class="card"><div class="label">The decided case (<i>aṣl</i>)</div><p class="small" style="margin:0">A seller keeps an animal unmilked for days so that its udder looks full. The Prophet (peace be upon him) forbade it; the buyer may milk, then keep the animal or return it. <span class="tiny">Sahih al-Bukhārī 2148; Sahih Muslim 1515</span></p></div></div>
<div class="col"><div class="card amber"><div class="label" style="color:#B25E00">The cause (<i>ʿilla</i>)</div><p class="small" style="margin:0">The seller creates a false appearance of a quality that sets the price, and the buyer relies on it.</p></div></div>
<div class="col"><div class="card teal"><div class="label">The new case (<i>farʿ</i>)</div><p class="small" style="margin:0">The maker hides a limit on the product&#8217;s life; the price was set for a life the product does not have, and the buyer relies on the silence.</p></div></div></div>
<div class="bar" style="margin-top:24px"><b>Ruling carried over:</b> forbidden, with the buyer&#8217;s option to rescind or be compensated.</div>
<ul class="b small" style="margin-top:20px">
<li>What the udder hides is <b>yield</b>. What the design hides is <b>life</b>. Both are qualities the buyer pays for and cannot see.</li>
<li>The texts forbid the <i>appearance</i>, not the animal. The cause is therefore not special to livestock.</li>
<li>No new principle is introduced. The old principle meets a new object.</li></ul>
<p class="tiny" style="margin-top:6px">'''+chip("strong")+''' Analogy from sound texts, plus the general texts on the previous slides.</p>''')

slide("Principle 1 · Consent", "Where the principle stops, and the best objection",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col"><div class="label">Boundaries: no concealment where</div><ul class="b small">
<li><b>The limit was disclosed</b> in plain words, before the sale, where a buyer would see it.</li>
<li><b>The buyer knew it and accepted it.</b> He then has no option to rescind. <span class="tiny">(TDV, &#8220;Ayıp&#8221;)</span> A limit any ordinary buyer would see at once is not hidden.</li>
<li><b>The limit is the known norm</b> of that kind of product, so no ordinary buyer expects more (Mecelle 41&#8211;42: custom counts only when it is settled and prevailing).</li></ul>
<p class="small"><b>Conditions that must hold for the ruling to bind:</b> the maker knew; the buyer did not; the shortfall is material; the buyer relied on the ordinary expectation.</p></div>
<div class="col"><div class="card dash"><div class="q">Objection</div><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 12px 0">&#8220;The buyer could have asked. It is his duty to check.&#8221;</p>
<div class="q" style="color:#0E6E6A">Reply</div><p class="small" style="margin:0">Where the matter is one the buyer needs to know, silence is itself a statement (Mecelle 67). And in the grain-heap case the owner is blamed for not showing the wet grain although the buyer could not see it. The duty lies on the one who knows, and a hidden limit is exactly what the buyer cannot find by looking.</p></div></div></div>''')

# ---------------- PRINCIPLE 2 : HARM
slide("Principle 2 · Harm", "Gain may not be bought with another&#8217;s loss",
'''<div class="card dash"><b>Imagine</b> a factory owner who saves money by letting his waste run into his neighbors&#8217; wells. The land is his and the profit is his. Does that make it right?</div>
<div class="cols" style="margin-top:20px"><div class="col"><div class="label">The norm</div><div class="bar" style="font-size:27px"><b>N2.</b> No one may gain by another&#8217;s loss without a right to do so.</div>
<p class="small" style="margin-top:16px">A &#8220;right&#8221; here is a ground that survives questioning: the other&#8217;s informed consent, a real necessity, or an entitlement that does not itself take another&#8217;s right. The defense tree later lists all of them.</p></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">Why it applies here</div><ul class="b small">
<li>The buyer loses the part of the price that bought nothing (Principle 1).</li>
<li>He also loses time and effort: he must buy again, earlier than he planned.</li>
<li>The maker&#8217;s gain comes <i>from</i> that loss: the sale is brought forward, or the repair market is kept.</li></ul>
<p class="small"><b>The test of connection:</b> remove the maker&#8217;s choice. If the buyer would not have lost, and the maker would not have gained, the harm is the maker&#8217;s doing.</p></div></div>''')

slide("Principle 2 · Harm", "Proof: the harm is real, caused, and without a right",
'''<ol class="prem">
<li data-n="2.1">The usable life of a product the buyer owns has value to him. <span class="tiny">(he paid for it)</span></li>
<li data-n="2.2">A choice by the maker removes part of that life, or part of the owner&#8217;s power to keep it. <span class="tiny">(definition; F1)</span></li>
<li data-n="2.3">But for that choice the owner would have kept it. <span class="tiny">(test of connection)</span></li>
<li data-n="2.4">The owner did not consent to the loss. <span class="tiny">(F2, Principle 1)</span></li>
<li data-n="2.5">The maker gains, or at least keeps, a market that the loss opens or protects. <span class="tiny">(replacement, repair monopoly)</span></li>
<li data-n="2.6">Absent consent, necessity or an entitlement, a gain made this way is without right. <span class="tiny">(N2; defenses tested later)</span></li>
<li class="concl" data-n="2.7">Therefore the choice is harm, and harm is to be removed and made good. <span style="float:right">'''+chip("strong")+'''</span></li></ol>
<p class="small" style="margin-top:14px">Step 2.5 is not needed for the wrong itself: a cost-cutting maker who gains nothing from the shortened life still harms. It is needed only to answer the claim that the harm is a by-product.</p>''',
"Strong rather than certain because step 2.6 depends on the defense tree on later slides: the tree has to hold.")

slide("Principle 2 · Harm", "What the classical texts and the Mecelle say",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col"><div class="label">The text</div>
<p style="margin:0 0 6px 0"><span class="ar">لَا ضَرَرَ وَلَا ضِرَارَ</span></p>
<p class="small" style="margin:0 0 12px 0"><i class="t">There is to be neither harming nor reciprocating harm.</i></p>
<ul class="b small"><li>Sunan Ibn Mājah 2340 (ʿUbāda ibn al-Ṣāmit) and 2341 (Ibn ʿAbbās); Mālik, <i>al-Muwaṭṭaʾ</i>; al-Nawawī, Forty Ḥadīth no. 32 (Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī).</li>
<li><b>Grade, stated honestly:</b> al-Nawawī calls it <i>ḥasan</i> through several chains. al-Albānī calls both <i>ṣaḥīḥ</i>; Shuʿayb al-Arnaʾūṭ calls both <i>ṣaḥīḥ li-ghayrihi</i>; Darussalam and Zubair ʿAlī Zaʾī call both weak.</li>
<li>The Qur&#8217;an rejects harm of this shape elsewhere: <i class="t">&#8220;do not retain them to harm them&#8221;</i> (2:231).</li></ul></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">The Mecelle&#8217;s maxims</div><ul class="b small">
<li><b>19.</b> No harm, and no answering harm with harm.</li>
<li><b>20.</b> Harm is to be removed.</li>
<li><b>30.</b> Repelling a mischief comes before gaining a benefit.</li>
<li><b>87&#8211;88.</b> Burdens go with benefits: the one who gains bears the cost.</li>
<li><b>92.</b> A direct actor is liable even without intent.</li>
<li><b>93.</b> One who only causes the loss is liable only if he intended it.</li></ul>
<div class="bar amber" style="margin-top:12px;font-size:21px">The word <i>planned</i> supplies the intent that art. 93 asks for.</div></div></div>''',
"Gradings: islamtics.com (Ibn Mājah 2340, 2341) and sunnah.com&#8217;s Nawawī-40 entry as reported in search summaries; quran.com for 2:231. Mecelle: Berki/Balcı text.")

slide("Principle 2 · Harm", "Where the principle stops, and the best objection",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col"><div class="label">Direct actor or cause?</div><ul class="b small">
<li><b>A2</b> (an update changes the owner&#8217;s device): the maker is the <i>direct actor</i>. Liable even without intent (art. 92).</li>
<li><b>A1, B, C</b> (a design, a withdrawal, a closed repair path): the maker is the <i>cause</i>. Liable if intended (art. 93), and planned obsolescence is by definition intended.</li></ul>
<div class="label" style="margin-top:14px">Boundaries</div><ul class="b small">
<li>The harm must be an actual shortfall against a reasonable expectation, not a lost chance of profit.</li>
<li>Natural aging is not harm done by anyone. Only the part caused by the maker&#8217;s choice counts.</li></ul></div>
<div class="col"><div class="card dash"><div class="q">Objection</div><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 12px 0">&#8220;All products lose value over time. Where is the harm?&#8221;</p>
<div class="q" style="color:#0E6E6A">Reply</div><p class="small" style="margin:0">The claim is not that products age. It is that a <i>choice</i> shortened the life, and the test of connection separates the two: without the choice, the owner keeps the product. Aging that no one chose passes the test and is outside the claim; a firmware limit or a sealed part does not pass it.</p></div></div></div>''')

# ---------------- PRINCIPLE 3 : OWNERSHIP
slide("Principle 3 · Ownership", "To own is to be able to use and to maintain",
'''<div class="card dash"><b>Imagine</b> you buy a car, but the dealer keeps a switch that stops it unless he approves each repair. Did you buy a car, or the right to ask for one?</div>
<div class="cols" style="margin-top:20px"><div class="col"><div class="label">The norm</div><div class="bar" style="font-size:27px"><b>N3.</b> A buyer who has paid the price owns the thing and alone decides how it is used, repaired and disposed of.</div></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">Why you already hold it</div><ul class="b small">
<li>If the seller keeps the decisions, he never sold: he leased, and should have called it a lease and priced it as one.</li>
<li>If a thing you paid for can be stopped, slowed or locked by someone else at will, then &#8220;yours&#8221; means nothing in practice.</li>
<li>The word <i>sale</i> promises more than a license to use.</li></ul></div></div>''')

slide("Principle 3 · Ownership", "Proof: remote control of a sold thing needs the owner&#8217;s permission",
'''<ol class="prem">
<li data-n="3.1">A sale transfers ownership of the thing and of its benefit to the buyer. <span class="tiny">(what &#8220;full ownership&#8221; means in the fiqh)</span></li>
<li data-n="3.2">The owner may use, alter, repair, consume and dispose of what he owns as he wishes, short of harming others. <span class="tiny">(Mecelle 1192, 1197)</span></li>
<li data-n="3.3">No one may act in another&#8217;s property without his permission, nor take it without a lawful ground. <span class="tiny">(Mecelle 95&#8211;97)</span></li>
<li data-n="3.4">What the seller has completed on his side he may not undo. <span class="tiny">(Mecelle 100)</span></li>
<li data-n="3.5">Slowing, disabling or locking a sold device, or closing its repair, is acting in the owner&#8217;s property or undoing a completed sale.</li>
<li class="concl" data-n="3.6">Therefore it is forbidden unless the owner gave a real, informed permission. <span style="float:right">'''+chip("strong")+'''</span></li></ol>
<p class="small" style="margin-top:14px">The text of 3.4 is Mecelle 100: <i class="t">whoever tries to undo what is completed on his side, his effort is rejected.</i></p>''',
"Mecelle 95&#8211;97, 100: Berki/Balcı text. 1192 and 1197: as cited in TDV İslâm Ansiklopedisi, &#8220;Mülkiyet&#8221;. Strong rather than certain: step 3.5 classifies acts, and 3.2 has limits discussed on the next slide.")

slide("Principle 3 · Ownership", "The contract defense, and what the schools say about conditions",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col"><div class="label">The defender&#8217;s best text</div>
<p class="small" style="margin:0 0 8px 0"><i class="t">Muslims are bound by their conditions</i>, except a condition that makes the lawful unlawful or the unlawful lawful. <span class="tiny">Sunan Abī Dāwūd 3594; Jāmiʿ al-Tirmidhī 1352 (<i>ḥasan ṣaḥīḥ</i>)</span></p>
<p class="small">The text is real, and it binds. The question is what it covers: a condition the buyer <b>knew</b> and <b>freely accepted</b>. A hidden term is not a condition he accepted.</p>
<div class="label">The same texts, the stricter reading</div>
<p class="small"><i class="t">Whoever stipulates a condition that is not in God&#8217;s Book, it is void.</i> <span class="tiny">Sahih al-Bukhārī 2155 (Barīra)</span></p></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">The schools on restrictive conditions</div><table style="font-size:19px"><tr><th>School</th><th>View, in short</th></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Ḥanafī</td><td>Classes conditions as valid, defective or void. A condition that the buyer may not resell is <b>void</b>; the sale stands.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Shāfiʿī</td><td>Leans to restricting conditions in contracts.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="k">Mālikī, Ḥanbalī</td><td>Lean to freedom: conditions stand unless something forbids them (Ibn Taymiyya: freedom is the rule).</td></tr></table>
<div class="bar" style="margin-top:14px;font-size:21px"><b>Our ruling holds under every school:</b> a <i>concealed</i> restriction is invalid in the strict schools and is no condition at all in the broad ones, because it was never known or accepted.</div></div></div>''',
"Tirmidhī and Abū Dāwūd texts and grades checked on prophetmuhammad.com; Bukhārī 2155 likewise. Schools: TDV İslâm Ansiklopedisi, &#8220;Şart (fürû-i fıkıh)&#8221;.")

slide("Principle 3 · Ownership", "Where the principle stops, and the best objection",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col"><div class="label">Boundaries</div><ul class="b small">
<li>The owner&#8217;s rights end where they harm others (Mecelle 1197, per TDV). He may not use a device to hurt a neighbor.</li>
<li>An owner may <b>choose</b> a restriction: automatic security updates, a repair partner, a lease. The ruling concerns restrictions he did not know and did not choose.</li>
<li>Where the maker really keeps ownership, he may keep control. Then the price must be a rent and the word must be <i>lease</i> (<i class="t">ijāra</i>).</li></ul></div>
<div class="col"><div class="card dash"><div class="q">Objection</div><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 12px 0">&#8220;Software is licensed, not sold. The device is only the carrier.&#8221;</p>
<div class="q" style="color:#0E6E6A">Reply</div><p class="small" style="margin:0">In a contract, meaning counts, not wording (Mecelle 3). If the buyer pays a price for permanent possession of a device that cannot work without the software, the contract is a sale of a working device, whatever the label says. A label cannot turn a sale into a lease after the money has changed hands.</p></div></div></div>''')

# ---------------- PRINCIPLE 4 : WASTE
slide("Principle 4 · Waste", "Usable value may not be destroyed: offered as support only",
'''<div class="cols"><div class="col"><div class="card dash"><b>Imagine</b> a shopkeeper who cracks every plate he sold, a month after the sale. Nobody gains from the broken plates except the one who sells new ones.</div>
<div class="label" style="margin-top:20px">The norm</div><div class="bar" style="font-size:25px"><b>N4.</b> Usable value may not be destroyed for no benefit.</div></div>
<div class="col"><div class="label">What the texts give, and what they do not</div><ul class="b small">
<li><i class="t">&#8220;Do not spend wastefully. Indeed, the wasteful are brothers of the devils.&#8221;</i> Qur&#8217;an 17:26&#8211;27.</li>
<li>The Prophet said that God <b>dislikes</b> for us <i>wasting wealth</i> (<i class="t">iḍāʿat al-māl</i>). Sahih al-Bukhārī 2408 and Sahih Muslim 593. Al-Bukhārī places it under the chapter heading <i>&#8220;What is forbidden of wasting wealth.&#8221;</i></li>
<li>But the hadith&#8217;s verb is <i>dislikes</i>, not <i>forbids</i> (one Muslim narration says <i>forbade</i>), and the classical discussion is mostly about spending one&#8217;s <i>own</i> wealth. The jurists differ on whether it is forbidden or disliked.</li></ul>
<p class="small">'''+chip("support")+''' <b>We do not rest the ruling on this.</b> The three earlier norms carry the proof. N4 adds weight where the destruction is chosen, serves no one but the destroyer, and is repeated at scale.</p></div></div>''',
"Wording and gradings checked on prophetmuhammad.com (Bukhārī 2408, Muslim 593) and quran.com (17:27); TDV İslâm Ansiklopedisi, &#8220;İsraf&#8221; for the jurists&#8217; difference.")
